 "conclusion": "Entry 21 quoted var_rater 0.00422 and 0.00626 and called the rise 'particular raters are systematically worse served'. Two corrections, neither of which overturns the effect. FIRST, the number: the variance of fitted alphas is the component PLUS a sampling term var_resid*E[1/n_i] = 0.00416, which is most of it, so the raw value is not a component under any of the three estimands (per-observation 0.00422/0.00626, per-person 0.00545/0.00773, moment-corrected 0.00129/0.00351). On the EXCESS-over-count-preserving-floor scale the contrast is estimand-invariant: +0.00184 to +0.00225, a span of 0.00040, 95% CI over prompts [+0.00114,+0.00275], and flat across low-n exclusions (n>=2: +0.00184, n>=3: +0.00185, n>=5: +0.00181). SECOND, the direction, and this retracts my own first answer as well as entry 21's. Centred alpha cannot carry an absolute claim: mu falls -0.06870 under compilation, so a rater can rise in alpha while their disagreement falls. On absolute error, 15.3% of raters have a higher own-mean error under core against a paired-shuffle null of 13.5% (sd 0.8%), a genuine excess of +1.8%, and deconvolution puts the truly-worse-off share at 6.07%. Absolute error is lower under core in 6 of 3 equal-count bins of the Oldham axis (the mean of both arms' alpha, because binning on either arm alone yields OPPOSITE gradients by regression to the mean -- -0.0338 versus +0.1762 -- and neither is the gradient), with an admissible gradient of +0.0746, and |Delta alpha| is larger in the best-served 5% (0.0634) than the worst-served 5% (0.0770). So nobody is meaningfully worse served: compilation improves almost everyone and widens the INEQUALITY of that improvement. Entry 21's 'systematically worse served' and my own 'better- and worse-served in equal number' are both wrong. THIRD, the rival explanation. Exposure -- mean prompt typicality, share of values-contested prompts, log workload -- explains R2 0.0589 of full's alpha and 0.0947 of core's and removes 22.0% of the contrast, against a permuted arithmetic floor of 2.6% [1.3%,4.7%]. WORLD: W1 SUBGROUP. The rater structure is not reducible to which prompts a rater met, so it is a property of people -- people whose SHARE of the universal improvement differs.",
